Requested by Max, 2026-09-01: any compiler output from the game (gold fixtures etc.) must be regenerable programmatically, so a whole suite can be rerun and results regenerated when AngelScript/Openplanet updates land. Expected cadence: a few times a year — optimise for auditability, not speed.

INVENTORY of engine-derived artifacts as of filing:
1. docs/probes/*.tsv — 8 corpora, each already naming its generating script (probe_enum_corpus.py, probe_export_binding.py, probe_shared_modifiers.py, probe_ns_binding.py, gen_b044_probe.py, issue_repro_game.py) with provenance in docs/probes/README.md. Individually regenerable today; NO single runner exists.
2. tests/fixtures/typedb/ (OpenplanetCore.json, OpenplanetNext.json, Openplanet.h) and tests/fixtures/typedb-versions/{1.29.5,1.29.14} — game dumps. Verify during implementation whether a regeneration script exists or this is a manual export; document or script it. (Dump carries no deprecation metadata — GH #18 context.)
3. Hand-transcribed measured tables in source comments/tests (10+ files incl. builtins.rs, repr.rs, interface_impl.rs — e.g. B112's spacing table, 'OP 1.29.5, live engine'). Not programmatically regenerable today.

DELIVERABLE:
a. One command (suggest 'just regen-golds') that enumerates every committed engine-derived artifact, reruns its generator against the live game (RemoteBuild port 30000 / tm-control-mcp, as the probes already do), writes fresh outputs, and prints a per-artifact drift report vs committed versions. Nothing auto-commits — a human reviews the diff and commits with a version-stamped message.
b. A checked-in manifest mapping artifact -> generator command -> game+Openplanet version last run -> row count, so the runner is data-driven and a new corpus cannot be added without registering its regeneration path.
c. typedb regeneration documented or scripted (implementer's call which).
d. Design decision for the implementer, deliberately not pre-decided here: in-source hand-transcribed tables either (i) migrate their ground truth into generated fixtures the tests read, or (ii) stay manual but are enumerated in the manifest with a manual-re-verify flag so a game-update run lists them as UNCHECKED rather than silently skipping them. (ii) is an acceptable v1.
e. The runner inherits the repo's probe discipline, mandatorily: selftests/nonvacuous checks run first and a probe that cannot discriminate FAILS the regen rather than overwriting golds with AMBIGUOUS; four-valued build_status per B118 (NOT-RUN is never a verdict); no stranded fixture in the game Plugins dir per B077.
f. Every regenerated artifact records game + Openplanet version and date; the runner enforces the stamp, not convention.

CONSTRAINTS: needs a live Trackmania + Openplanet session; runtime dominated by the b052 (1901 rows) and b053 (2989 rows) corpora.
COORDINATION: probe harness is largely the peer fleet's construction (B068/B077/B097/B118 guard chain); unclaimed — either fleet may take it; schedule AFTER B122 (repo-wide fmt commit) to avoid churn.

COORDINATOR APPEND (2026-09-01, post-acceptance): peer fleet (claude-mortar-vicar-eqog) has taken this ticket. Three spec refinements agreed via c2c, binding on the implementer:

1. MANIFEST GAINS A THIRD COLUMN: the QUESTION each artifact answers, alongside artifact -> generator -> version -> rowcount. Rationale (measured twice tonight): B118 and B123 both produced probes whose OUTPUT was right while their stated REASON was wrong; a regen preserves outputs and cannot re-derive intent, so the question field is the one that goes stale silently. Record it explicitly.

2. SELFTEST GATING TIGHTENED: per-guard KILL evidence, not suite-level green and not positive-control pairs. B118's lane had 4 of 9 guards vacuous with all four control pairs passing — earlier chain checks had already decided each case. The regen gate requires: (a) each guard dies under its own one-axis mutation and survives the others, and (b) an OVER-CONDEMN mutation — a probe that fails everything must FAIL the gate, since a guard that fires on both halves is as broken as one that fires on neither. Working models: fleet teardown selftest (21/21 mutations caught) and run_batch verdict chain (10/10).

3. ENGINE METHOD CONSTNESS IS IN SCOPE FROM DAY ONE: as of B124/B125, 301 of 1002 Core method decls parse a trailing ' const' from the typedb dumps (previously hardwired false — 'void M()' and 'void M() const' rendered identically). The drift report can now see an engine method gaining/losing its const qualifier between Openplanet versions, which is exactly the ABI-change class this ticket exists to catch. Manifest it.

On the open design question (in-source hand-measured tables): peer's instinct is v1 enumerates them manually rather than auto-regenerating — a hand-measured table encodes human scope judgement that silent regeneration would destroy — but will MEASURE the inventory before committing, and append findings here. Enumeration at minimum makes them answer to the drift report as UNCHECKED rather than invisible.